{"study_accession":"PRJNA1320723","secondary_study_accession":"SRP617875","title":"Longitudinal analysis of viral and host RNA in blood and saliva during controlled human dengue virus 3 infections","description":"Dengue viruses are blood-borne pathogens transmitted by mosquitoes. Dengue cases have recently exploded worldwide, but one issue with tracking this unfolding epidemic is that diagnosis requires blood. Nine participants were enrolled in clinical trial NCT04298138 to receive an inoculation with dengue virus (DENV-3). Matched blood and saliva specimens were collected over 10 days. Uniquely, our participants are synchronized with respect to timing, dose, and viral variant, a situation that is virtually impossible to achieve outside of a controlled challenge study. These controlled samples demonstrated that dengue virus genomes became detectable by RT-qPCR in blood at 3.4 days post infection and in saliva at 5.0 days post infection (for eight participants). Thus, future saliva-based tests for dengue virus could make diagnostics and surveillance more accessible globally, possibly even through development of self-tests. We also used this unique sample set to identify human genes from which transcripts are increased upon dengue infection.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":16,"study_labels":[{"level":"study","event_code":"VIRAL_CHALLENGE","certainty":"confirmed","delta_days":0.0,"delta_bucket":"D0","time_precision":"day","stage":"teacher","confidence":1.0,"evidence":"Nine participants were enrolled in clinical trial NCT04298138 to receive an inoculation with dengue virus (DENV-3).","model_version":"teacher:mlx:mlx-community/Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct-4bit"},{"level":"study","event_code":"VIRAL_CHALLENGE","certainty":"confirmed","delta_days":0.0,"delta_bucket":"D0","time_precision":"day","stage":"teacher","confidence":1.0,"evidence":"Uniquely, our participants are synchronized with respect to timing, dose, and viral variant, a situation that is virtually impossible 